title Three files state one desk's arity, and nothing compared them
prompt the fleet baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the language lane
rota lap 4599, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; hand-advanced past rows 0, 2, 3 and 4, which peers read today. Air asks where each thing ends, so the lap walked the Glow run contract's fence line and pressed on every post: the one that gave was arity.
think Opened cold. 245 rostered guards, 244 green, 3 red -- readme_metrics, index_row_bound, and standing_equipment answering red_self behind them.
think Reds first. index_row_bound: two rows of today's shelf stood out of order, the ordinary shared-prepend-under-rebase fault. Repaired by tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh, witness GREEN.
think readme_metrics reads DRIFT on the witness count. That is the generated-page debt the pre-commit hook pays, and the card's own directive is to regenerate after git add, so it rides in this lap's own commit rather than a repair of its own.
think Air's question against my lane: where is a boundary drawn twice? REDS %532 answered it in its own body. It named three hand-written enumerations of the Glow desk corpus -- arity in the worker, runnability in each desk's head, coverage in the elder witness -- repaired the second and third, and left arity underived.
think Measured first. glow/bin/glow_run --sample-argv over all 352 desks in glow/gen: 135 lower on the argv road, 115 of those read at least one argv value, 217 decline. Every one of the 352 lowers on the WELCOME road except eight -- the five -refuse desks doing their job and the three data fixtures %532 already named. The corpus is clean.
think The comparison I set out to make could not be made: the worker stated its arity only as the shape of what it refused, a nest of test "$NARGS" -eq N, readable only by parsing shell or by probing with wrong counts.
think So the repair came first. arity_accepts() states the accepted counts once, per stem; the membership test is the whole check; --arity prints the same list. One statement, two readers -- the check and the meter.
think Then the fourth enumeration surfaced. glow_desk_run_scan.sh runs 46 sampled desks "on the sample each declares for itself" -- a ::  Sample: line in each desk's own head. That is exactly the fourth hand-written enumeration %532 predicted in its own words, and it arrived without anyone noticing the prediction had come true.
think Three statements, all compared: 46 sampled desks, zero disagreement. The same perfect, complete, uninstrumented agreement %532 found among its own three.
obs The lowering reading has to take the MAXIMUM argv gate, not the first. A tag desk's emitted program checks argv.len < 2 for the tag, branches, and checks < 3 inside the mint arm -- so reading the first gate called gate-xact-tag, gate-xfer-tag and their barket twins split when nothing was wrong. Planted in the control and mutation-proven: reverting to head -1 reds three legs.
obs Two shapes of one boundary stand here and only one is exact. The worker demands a COUNT; the emitted program demands a FLOOR and ignores anything past it. Named for Keaton rather than gated, since nothing reaches a built desk binary except through the worker today.
obs sample_unpermitted is gated rather than reported because a Sample line the worker would refuse is a promise the tree cannot keep -- the desk says how to run it and the one runner declines the argument by name, and neither file can see it alone.
obs arity_unanswered gates the instrument-cannot-answer case. A silent accepts= would let every comparison below read green by having nothing to compare.
loom witness=glow_desk_arity wall_s=9 desks=352 permitted=46 declared=46 lowering_read=46 faults=0
